(HF, "Chronic amiodarone + fibrosis on CT + restrictive PFTs → suspect {{c1::amiodarone pulmonary toxicity}}",
     "*Easy to miss when pulmonary edema explains the hypoxemia"),

# ---------------- Pt 2: low-output shock, dual RAAS blockade, AKI
(SH, "<b>ACEi + ARB together</b> vs a single agent: ↑ risk of {{c1::AKI}} and {{c1::hyperkalemia}}; the combination should be {{c2::avoided}}",
     "*AKI RR ~1.48, hyperkalemia RR ~1.97 vs single agent"),
(SH, "How <b>RAAS blockade</b> lowers GFR: {{c1::efferent arteriolar dilation → ↓ glomerular filtration pressure}}",
     "*Low aldosterone → ↑ K<br><br>*Vasodilation → ↓ SVR<br><br>*Add a loop diuretic (↓ preload) and a beta-blocker (no HR rescue) on a fixed-low stroke volume → CO falls"),
(SH, "Renal perfusion pressure = {{c1::MAP − CVP}}",
     "*Low MAP (shock) or high CVP (congestion) both drop renal perfusion"),
(SH, "<b>FeUrea</b> {{c1::&lt;35%}} suggests a prerenal state and stays useful when the patient is on {{c2::diuretics}}",
     "*FeNa is unreliable on diuretics<br><br>*Prolonged prerenal state → ATN"),
(SH, "Why hypotension spirals in <b>ischemic cardiomyopathy with residual severe CAD</b>: ↓ MAP → {{c1::↓ coronary perfusion → ischemia → weaker pump → ↓ MAP}}",
     ""),
(SH, "First-line vasopressor in <b>cardiogenic shock with hypotension</b>: {{c1::norepinephrine}}",
     "*α → ↑ SVR/MAP; β1 → some inotropy<br><br>*MAP &gt;65 is the usual target (not well proven); follow perfusion too: UOP, lactate, extremities<br><br>*Lowest dose, shortest time. Tradeoffs: ↑ afterload on a weak LV, arrhythmia, ↑ O2 demand"),
(SH, "<b>Pure α-agonists alone</b> (<i>phenylephrine, midodrine</i>) are discouraged in <b>cardiogenic shock</b> because they cause {{c1::↑ afterload + reflex bradycardia → ↓ cardiac output}}",
     ""),
(SH, "<b>MIDAS trial</b> (midodrine 20 mg q8h added to IV vasopressors in the ICU): {{c1::no faster vasopressor liberation}} and more {{c2::bradycardia}}",
     ""),
(SH, "Label contraindications to <b>midodrine</b>: {{c1::severe organic heart disease}}, {{c2::acute renal disease}}, {{c3::urinary retention}}",
     "*Active metabolite is renally cleared; renal starting dose 2.5 mg"),
(SH, "Drug class of <b>tamsulosin</b>: {{c1::α1 blocker}} → in shock it {{c2::lowers BP and opposes norepinephrine/midodrine}}",
     "*Hold in hypotension<br><br>*If retention is the worry → bladder scan + renal US first"),
(SH, "<b>RHC patterns in undifferentiated hypotension</b>:<br><br>Low RA/PCWP = {{c1::hypovolemia → volume}}<br>Low CI + high SVR = {{c2::pump failure → inotrope}}<br>Low SVR = {{c3::vasodilatory (drug/sepsis) → vasopressor}}",
     ""),
(SH, "Fluid strategy in <b>\"cold and dry\"</b> low-output shock with a very low EF: {{c1::small boluses (<i>e.g. 500 mL</i>), reassess after each, stop at congestion}}",
     "*Dry exam + normal RA pressure → preload test is reasonable<br><br>*Start the pressor early; no large-volume resuscitation"),
(SH, "<b>DOREMI trial</b> (milrinone vs dobutamine in cardiogenic shock): {{c1::no difference in outcomes}}",
     "*Milrinone: renally cleared → poor fit in severe AKI<br><br>*Dobutamine: arrhythmia, ischemia with residual CAD"),
(SH, "Maximum <b>rosuvastatin</b> dose when <b>CrCl &lt;30</b> (not on dialysis): {{c1::10 mg/day}}",
     "*Accumulation → myopathy/rhabdomyolysis"),
(SH, "<b>Metformin</b> must be held in AKI because of {{c1::lactic acidosis}}",
     "*Check lactate"),
(SH, "Anticoagulant of choice for a recent PE when there is <b>severe AKI</b> or procedures are likely: {{c1::unfractionated heparin infusion}}",
     "*Short-acting, titratable, reversible, not renally cleared<br><br>*DOACs unreliable in AKI<br><br>*PE &lt;3 months → anticoagulation continues"),
(SH, "Hypotension + dry exam in a patient on <b>anticoagulant + P2Y12 inhibitor</b> with iron-deficiency anemia → must exclude {{c1::GI bleed (check Hgb)}}",
     "*Also on the list: stent thrombosis/ACS (troponin, ECG), recurrent PE (RV size/function)<br><br>*P2Y12 inhibitor cannot be stopped within the first month after a stent"),

# ---------------- Pt 3: Ludwig's angina, intubated
(LU, "<b>Ludwig's angina</b> = rapidly spreading cellulitis of the {{c1::bilateral submandibular, sublingual and submental}} spaces",
     "*Usual source: mandibular molar infection (2nd/3rd)<br><br>*Tongue pushed up and back → oropharyngeal + supraglottic obstruction"),
(LU, "Management order in <b>Ludwig's angina</b>: {{c1::secure the airway}} → {{c2::early surgical drainage (formal I&amp;D)}} + IV antibiotics",
     "*Antibiotics alone → airway compromise 26.3% vs 2.9% with early surgical drainage<br><br>*Drainage also gives cultures"),
(LU, "<b>Gas</b> in a deep neck collection means {{c1::anaerobic/polymicrobial infection or necrotizing infection}}",
     "*Imaging alone cannot rule out necrotizing fasciitis<br><br>*Clinical signs: crepitus, skin necrosis, spreading pain/erythema, pressor need → broaden + add clindamycin (toxin suppression), urgent OR"),
(LU, "Deep neck infection descends to the mediastinum mostly via the {{c1::retropharyngeal \"danger\" space}}; mortality once mediastinal is {{c2::10–40%}}",
     "*Descending necrotizing mediastinitis<br><br>*CXR is not enough; CT neck + chest is the test<br><br>*Sedated patient cannot report chest pain → low threshold to scan"),
(LU, "Comorbidity strongly tied to multi-space spread and <b>descending mediastinitis</b> in deep neck infection: {{c1::poorly controlled diabetes}}",
     "*Hyperglycemia → neutrophil dysfunction<br><br>*Afebrile + normal WBC is not reassurance when recent steroids + DM blunt the response"),
(LU, "Why a <b>passed SBT</b> does not clear extubation in Ludwig's angina: SBT tests {{c1::lungs/respiratory pump}}, not {{c1::upper airway patency}}",
     "*Extubate after source control + swelling down, cuff leak present, airway team at the bedside<br><br>*Reintubation through a swollen supraglottic airway could fail"),
(LU, "First-line empiric antibiotic class for <b>odontogenic deep neck infection</b>: {{c1::penicillin + β-lactamase inhibitor (<i>ampicillin-sulbactam</i>)}}",
     "*Covers oral strep, anaerobes, MSSA<br><br>*Ampicillin-sulbactam 3 g IV q6h; max sulbactam 4 g/day; CrCl 15–29 → q12h"),
(LU, "ICU sedation (PADIS): prefer {{c1::propofol or dexmedetomidine}} over {{c2::benzodiazepines}}; target {{c3::light sedation, analgesia first}}",
     "*Oversedated + hypotensive on triple infusion → stop midazolam first, then lower propofol<br><br>*Difficult airway: aim RASS −1 to −2, not 0 (self-extubation risk)"),
(LU, "Main hemodynamic side effects: <b>propofol</b> → {{c1::hypotension}}; <b>dexmedetomidine</b> → {{c2::bradycardia}}",
     "*Propofol infusion syndrome at high dose / long duration: metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalemia, arrhythmia, ↑ triglycerides"),
(LU, "Glucose target for a critically ill patient on an <b>insulin infusion</b>: {{c1::140–180}} mg/dL",
     "*Persistent glucose &gt;180 on sliding scale alone = inadequate → insulin infusion<br><br>*Check ketones + A1c"),
(LU, "Refractory hypotension after a <b>recent steroid course</b> → think {{c1::HPA-axis suppression}} → {{c2::cortisol / stress-dose hydrocortisone}}",
     ""),
(LU, "<b>Winter's formula</b>: expected pCO2 = {{c1::1.5 × HCO3 + 8 (± 2)}}",
     "*HCO3 18 → expected pCO2 35 ± 2<br><br>*Measured pCO2 above expected = added respiratory acidosis; below = added respiratory alkalosis"),
(LU, "Before calling oliguric AKI in a patient with an <b>external urine collection device</b>: {{c1::verify with a bladder scan (under-collection / retention)}}",
     ""),
(LU, "Lung-protective tidal volume: {{c1::6–8}} mL/kg of {{c2::ideal (predicted)}} body weight",
     "*P/F ratio = PaO2 ÷ FiO2 (as a fraction); e.g. 155 ÷ 0.4 = 388"),
